Apply the file:

kubectl apply -f redis-pvs.yaml

Verify the Persistent Volumes:
kubectl get pv

2. Creating a Redis Service
A Redis service is required to expose the Redis pods. In this step, we set up a headless service to allow Redis nodes to communicate with each other.

Step-by-Step:
Create a file called redis-cluster-service.yaml:
(use nano)
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: redis-cluster-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: redis
  ports:
    - name: client
      port: 6379
      targetPort: 6379
    - name: gossip
      port: 16379
      targetPort: 16379
  clusterIP: None  # Headless service for Redis cluster communication

Apply the file:
kubectl apply -f redis-cluster-service.yaml

Verify the service:
kubectl get svc

3. Configuring StatefulSets
StatefulSets are crucial for stateful applications like Redis, ensuring stable identities for pods across restarts. In this step, we configure a StatefulSet with 6 replicas, each having its own Persistent Volume Claim (PVC) and the necessary configuration for Redis.

Step-by-Step:
Create a file called redis-cluster-statefulset.yaml:
(use nano)
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: redis-cluster
spec:
  serviceName: redis-cluster-service
  replicas: 6
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: redis-cluster
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: redis
        image: redis:5.0.1-alpine
        command: ["/conf/update-node.sh", "redis-server", "/conf/redis.conf"]
        env:
        - name: POD_IP
          valueFrom:
            fieldRef:
              fieldPath: status.podIP
        ports:
        - name: client
          containerPort: 6379
        - name: gossip
          containerPort: 16379
        volumeMounts:
        - name: conf
          mountPath: /conf
          readOnly: false
        - name: data
          mountPath: /data
          readOnly: false
      volumes:
      - name: conf
        configMap:
          name: redis-cluster-configmap
          defaultMode: 0755
  volumeClaimTemplates:
  - metadata:
      name: data
    spec:
      accessModes: [ "ReadWriteOnce" ]
      resources:
        requests:
          storage: 1Gi
Apply the file:

kubectl apply -f redis-cluster-statefulset.yaml

Verify the pod status:

kubectl get pods -l app=redis-cluster

4. Creating the Redis Cluster

Finally, we use the redis-cli command to create the Redis cluster and set up replication across the Redis nodes.

Step-by-Step:
Execute the following command to create the Redis cluster:

kubectl exec -it redis-cluster-0 -- redis-cli --cluster create --cluster-replicas 1 $(kubectl get pods -l app=redis-cluster -o jsonpath='{range.items[*]}{.status.podIP}:6379 {end}')
This command will create the cluster with replication (1 replica per master node) based on the pod IPs of all Redis pods.

